#: ../../../platforms.rst:226
msgid "macOS"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:229
msgid "Path Helper"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:231
msgid ""
"macOS provides a `path helper <http://www.softec.lu/site/DevelopersCorner/"
"MasteringThePathHelper>`_, which by default configures paths in bash and "
"other POSIX or C  shells. Without including these paths, common tools "
"including those installed by Homebrew may be unavailable. See ``/etc/"
"profile`` for details on how it is done. To ensure the path helper is "
"invoked on xonsh (for all users), add the following to ``/etc/xonsh/"
"xonshrc``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:239
msgid "source-bash $(/usr/libexec/path_helper -s)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:243
msgid "To incorporate the whole functionality of ``/etc/profile``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:245
msgid "source-bash --seterrprevcmd \"\" /etc/profile"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:252
msgid "GNU Coreutils"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:254
msgid ""
"macOS ships with BSD versions of common utilities (``ls``, ``grep``, "
"``sed``, etc.) which have different flags and behaviour compared to the GNU "
"versions found on Linux. If you work across both platforms or prefer GNU "
"behaviour, install GNU coreutils and grep via Homebrew:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:259
msgid "@ brew install coreutils grep findutils gnu-sed gnu-tar gawk"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:263
msgid ""
"Homebrew installs GNU tools with a ``g`` prefix (e.g. ``gls``, ``ggrep``). "
"To use them without the prefix, add the GNU paths to your ``$PATH`` in your :"
"doc:`xonsh RC <xonshrc>`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:267
msgid ""
"brew_prefix = $(brew --prefix).strip()\n"
"gnu_paths = [\n"
"    f\"{brew_prefix}/opt/coreutils/libexec/gnubin\",\n"
"    f\"{brew_prefix}/opt/grep/libexec/gnubin\",\n"
"    f\"{brew_prefix}/opt/findutils/libexec/gnubin\",\n"
"    f\"{brew_prefix}/opt/gnu-sed/libexec/gnubin\",\n"
"    f\"{brew_prefix}/opt/gnu-tar/libexec/gnubin\",\n"
"]\n"
"for p in gnu_paths:\n"
"    if @.imp.os.path.isdir(p):\n"
"        $PATH.insert(0, p)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:281
msgid "After this, ``ls``, ``grep``, ``sed``, etc. will be the GNU versions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:285
msgid "Android / Termux"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:287
msgid ""
"Xonsh runs on Android via `Termux <https://termux.dev>`_ and similar "
"sandboxes (UserLAnd, proot-distro, Linux Deploy). The Android userland is "
"Linux-kernel-based but uses bionic libc, a non-FHS layout under ``$PREFIX``, "
"and the per-app filesystem sandbox — a few quirks follow from that."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:294
msgid "Installing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:296
msgid ""
"In a fresh Termux install, set up xonsh and a working bash completion "
"framework:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:299
msgid ""
"@ pkg install python git bash-completion man\n"
"@ pip install 'xonsh[full]'\n"
"@ xonsh"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:305
msgid ""
":doc:`xonsh RC <xonshrc>` lives at the usual ``~/.xonshrc``; everything else "
"(history, data dir, completions cache) lands under ``$XDG_DATA_HOME`` inside "
"``$PREFIX``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:310
msgid "Sandbox limitations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:312
msgid "Android disallows a few syscalls that desktop Linux takes for granted:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:314
msgid ""
"``os.listdir(\"/\")`` — denied. Globs that have to enumerate the FS root "
"return empty (e.g. ``ls /e<TAB>`` will not find ``/etc/``). Locate items by "
"absolute literal path (``/data/data/com.termux/...``) or relative paths "
"under ``$HOME`` / ``$PREFIX`` instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:318
msgid ""
"``os.tcsetpgrp`` — restricted, returns ``EACCES``. xonsh's pipeline manager "
"handles this gracefully (no controlling terminal handover), so subprocesses "
"still work; you just don't get bash-style job control on Android-only "
"sessions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:322
msgid ""
"``os.link`` — not exposed in Python on bionic. xonsh xoreutils ``cp -l`` and "
"similar features fall back to copying."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:326
msgid "Detecting Android in xonshrc"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:328
msgid ""
"from xonsh.platform import ON_ANDROID, ON_TERMUX\n"
"\n"
"if ON_ANDROID:\n"
"    # Anything that should be tweaked for the Android sandbox in\n"
"    # general — runs in Termux, UserLAnd, proot-distro alike.\n"
"    $XONSH_HISTORY_BACKEND = 'json'   # sqlite is fine but json\n"
"                                      # avoids the WAL lock on /sdcard\n"
"\n"
"if ON_TERMUX:\n"
"    # Termux-specific niceties.\n"
"    aliases['open'] = 'termux-open'\n"
"    aliases['share'] = 'termux-share'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:343
msgid ""
"Use ``ON_ANDROID`` for sandbox/bionic-aware code that should also fire under "
"non-Termux Android shells. Reserve ``ON_TERMUX`` for things that genuinely "
"depend on Termux's path layout."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:350
msgid ""
"After ``pkg install bash-completion``, completions for git, pip, ssh, ``--`` "
"long options, and so on work out of the box — xonsh probes ``$PREFIX/share/"
"bash-completion/bash_completion`` by default."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:354
msgid ""
"For commands xonsh itself provides (``cd``, ``rmdir``, …) and filesystem "
"path completion, the same case-insensitive, subsequence- and fuzzy-tier "
"behaviour described in :doc:`completers <completers>` applies on Android."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:361
msgid "Windows"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:364
msgid "Coreutils"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:366
msgid ""
"Windows ships none of the familiar Unix command-line utilities (``ls``, "
"``grep``, ``cat``, ``cp``, ``find``, ``sed``, …) by default. The options "
"below give you a working set, ordered roughly from most lightweight and "
"xonsh-native to most comprehensive."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:371
msgid ""
"**xonsh built-in coreutils.** A pure-Python xontrib that ships with xonsh "
"registers ``cat``, ``echo``, ``pwd``, ``tee``, ``tty``, ``umask``, "
"``uname``, ``uptime``, ``yes`` as aliases — no install, identical behaviour "
"on every platform:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:376
msgid "@ xontrib load coreutils"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:380
msgid ""
"``which`` is already wired by default. Add the line above to your :doc:"
"`xonsh RC <xonshrc>` to load it on every session."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:383
msgid ""
"`cmdix <https://pypi.org/project/cmdix>`_ — pure-Python implementation of a "
"much larger GNU-style set: ``ls``, ``cp``, ``mv``, ``rm``, ``find``, "
"``grep``, ``head``, ``tail``, ``wc``, ``sort``, ``uniq``, ``du``, ``df``, "
"``date``, ``base64``, and many more. Because it's Python it installs into "
"the same interpreter as xonsh and behaves identically across Windows, macOS, "
"and Linux:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:390
msgid "@ xpip install cmdix"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:394
msgid ""
"`uutils/coreutils <https://github.com/uutils/coreutils>`_ — modern Rust port "
"of GNU coreutils as a single multi-call binary (``coreutils.exe``) plus per-"
"command shims. Stays very close to the GNU originals, including locale and "
"Unicode handling:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:399
msgid "@ winget install uutils.coreutils"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:403
msgid ""
"`MSYS2 <https://www.msys2.org>`_ — pacman-based distribution shipping full "
"GNU coreutils, findutils, grep, sed, awk, tar, gzip, openssl, make, gcc/"
"clang, and thousands of other packages. The most comprehensive option. After "
"the base install, add the relevant ``usr/bin`` directory (e.g. ``C:"
"\\msys64\\usr\\bin``) to ``$PATH`` in your :doc:`xonsh RC <xonshrc>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:410
msgid ""
"`Git for Windows <https://gitforwindows.org>`_ ``usr/bin``. If Git is "
"already installed, ``C:\\Program Files\\Git\\usr\\bin`` already contains a "
"curated MinGW-w64 toolset (``ls``, ``grep``, ``sed``, ``awk``, ``find``, "
"``ssh``, ``curl``, ``tar``, ``gzip``, ``less``, …). The fastest way to get a "
"working Unix toolchain without installing anything extra:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:417
msgid ""
"git_usr_bin = r'C:\\Program Files\\Git\\usr\\bin'\n"
"if @.imp.os.path.isdir(git_usr_bin):\n"
"    $PATH.append(git_usr_bin)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:423
msgid ""
"`busybox-w32 <https://frippery.org/busybox>`_ — single-binary BusyBox port "
"covering 300+ commands in roughly 1 MB. Drop ``busybox.exe`` on ``$PATH`` "
"and call commands as ``busybox ls`` / ``busybox grep ...``, or symlink each "
"applet to ``busybox.exe`` so they resolve as bare names. Ideal for portable "
"USB or air-gapped setups."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:431
msgid "Windows Terminal"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:433
msgid ""
"If you are running a supported version of Windows (which is now Windows 10, "
"version 2004 or later), we recommend the Windows Terminal (``wt.exe``) "
"rather than the time-honored ``cmd.exe``.  This provides unicode rendering, "
"better ansi terminal compatibility and all the conveniences you expect from "
"the terminal application in other platforms."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../platforms.rst:438
msgid ""
"You can install it from the `Microsoft Store <https://www.microsoft.com/en-"
"us/p/windows-terminal/9n0dx20hk701>`_ or from `Github <https://github.com/"
"microsoft/terminal>`_."
msgstr ""

